  "part_of_speech": "noun, suru verb",
  "gloss": "deep thought; contemplation; meditation; pondering",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "deep thought; contemplation; meditation; pondering",
      "explanation": "A state of deep, absorbed thinking. A literary and formal word used in written language and narratives to describe someone lost in profound thought."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "24602_chinshi_ex1",
      "japanese": "しばらく{沈思|ちんし}してから{答|こた}えた。",
      "english": "After pondering for a while, I answered.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "24602_chinshi_ex2",
      "japanese": "{彼|かれ}は{窓|まど}の{外|そと}を{見|み}ながら{沈思|ちんし}{黙考|もっこう}していた。",
      "english": "He was deep in thought, gazing out the window in silent contemplation.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "24602_chinshi_ex3",
      "japanese": "{大|おお}きな{決断|けつだん}を{前|まえ}にして、{彼女|かのじょ}は{数日間|すうじつかん}{沈思|ちんし}を{続|つづ}けた{末|すえ}にようやく{結論|けつろん}を{出|だ}した。",
      "english": "Faced with a major decision, she continued to contemplate for several days before finally reaching a conclusion.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    }
  ],
  "notes": "A literary and formal word meaning to think deeply or be lost in thought. Often used in written language, novels, and formal essays. Frequently appears in the four-character compound {沈思黙考|ちんしもっこう} (silent contemplation).\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {沈思黙考|ちんしもっこう}: deep and silent contemplation\n- {沈思|ちんし}にふける: to be absorbed in contemplation\n- {沈思|ちんし}を{続|つづ}ける: to continue pondering\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {熟考|じゅっこう}: careful consideration — deliberate, thorough thinking before a decision\n- {瞑想|めいそう}: meditation — spiritual or mindfulness practice\n- {思索|しさく}: speculation, reflection — philosophical or intellectual pondering\n- {考|かんが}え{込|こ}む: to ponder, to think hard — more everyday, implies being stuck in thought",
